  "text": "Question eight. How do you present a finding to a non-technical stakeholder? Lead with the answer and what it means for the business, not the method. Put the takeaway in the title, and show one clear chart rather than a table. Say the caveats plainly, like this excludes refunds. End with what you recommend. The method goes in the appendix. Question nine. How do you explain a complex statistical finding in plain language? Anchor it to one number people can picture: customers who saw the new design were twelve percent more likely to buy. Drop the jargon. Instead of a ninety-five percent confidence interval, say we're highly confident the true lift is between eight and sixteen percent. Then tie it to the decision it informs. Question ten. How do you communicate uncertainty? Say what the number rests on, like the last ninety days. Give a range, not just a point. Name the assumptions, and how far the answer moves if they're wrong. And don't be falsely precise. Twelve point three seven percent is about twelve, when the range runs from eight to sixteen.",
